# UI Kit Demo

UI Kit Demo is a read-only reference tool for the generated UI kit
(`lib/{{ package }}/`). It shows the three dialog families a tool is most likely
to need, in the firm's own tokens, so a developer can see and copy the pattern.

## User workflow

1. Open any Revit document.
2. On **{{ tab_display }}**, in **{{ panel_display }}**, click **UI Kit Demo**.
3. Choose which selector to try (the chooser dialog, module M0).
4. Pick one view or several from a searchable list of up to 50 view names (the
   selection dialog, module M2-lite).
5. Read the outcome in the result dialog (module M1).

The tool exits without a dialog when no document is open. Canceling at any
dialog stops the tool; only canceling the selector shows a result.

## Technical design

- **Minimum pyRevit version:** 4.8.10
- **Document context:** any open document (it reads only the title and view names)
- **Revit API query:** `FilteredElementCollector(doc).OfClass(DB.View)`
- **User interface:** `{{ package }}.chooser_dialog`, `{{ package }}.selection_dialog`,
  `{{ package }}.result_dialog` (WPF, IronPython 2.7)
- **External dependencies:** none beyond pyRevit and the Revit API
- **Linked models:** not accessed

## Pattern for tools that change the model

Collect input, show the input dialog, run one short Transaction with rollback on
failure, then show the result dialog after the Transaction has finished. Never
leave a Transaction open while a dialog is on screen. This demo has no
Transaction at all.

## Risk and transactions

- **Risk classification:** Low / read-only
- **Modifies the Revit database:** No
- **Transaction:** None

## Support

Firm support: <{{ support_url }}>

## Troubleshooting

- **No dialog appears:** open a Revit document and run the tool again.
- **Text uses a fallback font:** the packaged fonts live under
  `lib/{{ package }}/fonts/`; reload the extension and confirm the folder exists.
- **The button is missing:** reload the extension or restart Revit after
  confirming that the extension is installed and enabled.

